```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
source(file.path(getOption("bootmakr.precompute.dir"), "helpers.R"))
```

A regression coefficient from observational data is only as credible as the
assumption that no important variable was left out. A sensitivity analysis
asks how strong an omitted variable would have to be to change the
conclusion. `sensemakr` (Cinelli & Hazlett, 2020) answers with a concrete
number: the coefficient you would have obtained had a variable as strong as
one of your observed covariates been omitted.

`bootmakr` adds inference to that number that does not rely on the
conventional OLS variance formula. It bootstraps the whole procedure, so the
standard error, confidence interval and *p*-value of the adjusted coefficient
remain usable when the errors are heteroskedastic or correlated within
clusters. This page shows a first analysis from start to finish. The same
steps in Stata are in
[Get started in Stata](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/stata.html).

## Installation

`bootmakr` is installed from GitHub. `sensemakr`, which it builds on, comes
from CRAN and is installed along with it.

```{r install, eval = FALSE}
# install.packages("remotes")
remotes::install_github("jespernwulff/bootmakr")

# to read the example data used on this page
install.packages("haven")
```

## The example

We use the data of Chen, Chittoor, and Vissa (2021), who ask whether CEOs
that belong to the family controlling the firm are paid more than
professional CEOs. The authors share their data on OSF, and `haven` reads
the file from there.

```{r data}
library(bootmakr)
library(haven)

osf  <- "https://osf.io/download/stk7y/?view_only=885d1ed5498c4b3c8a1194882e468286"
chen <- read_dta(osf)

ceo.model <- lg_ceopay ~ owner_ceo + ceo_tenure + PA_nic3_med + ceo_edu_dummy +
  lg_sales + firm_age + promoters_pct + institutions_pct +
  factor(year) + factor(nic_code_1digit)

coef(lm(ceo.model, data = chen))["owner_ceo"]
```

The data hold 2,011 firm-year observations of 277 Indian firms. `lg_ceopay`
is the logarithm of total CEO pay, `owner_ceo` indicates a family CEO, and
the remaining variables are controls: CEO tenure and education, firm sales
and age, ownership shares, and year and industry indicators. The coefficient
of `owner_ceo` is the published one (Model 2 of their Table 2). Family CEOs
earn more.

## A first analysis

Could an omitted variable explain this away? To answer, `sensemakr` needs a
*benchmark*: an observed covariate that sets the scale for the omitted
variable. We take the age of the firm and ask what the coefficient of
`owner_ceo` would be had a variable *as strong as* `firm_age` been omitted.

```{r first, cache = TRUE}
first <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                  benchmark_covariates = "firm_age",
                  cluster = "co_code",
                  seed = 123, progress = FALSE)
first
```

```{r first-numbers, include = FALSE}
b0 <- coef(lm(ceo.model, data = chen))[["owner_ceo"]]
r1 <- first$results
ob <- first$benchmark_strength$observed
im <- first$benchmark_strength$implied
```

The call names the model, the treatment (`treat`), the benchmark
(`benchmark_covariates`) and, because observations of the same firm are not
independent, the variable that identifies the clusters to resample
(`cluster`). Everything else is left at its default: an omitted variable
exactly as strong as the benchmark (`kd = 1`) and 1,000 bootstrap
replications. `seed` makes the result reproducible, and `progress = FALSE`
switches off the progress bar, which is useful on screen but not in a
document. The run takes about 20 seconds.

### Reading the output

**The adjusted estimate.** Had a variable as strong as `firm_age` been
omitted, the coefficient of `owner_ceo` would be `r num(r1$estimate, 3)`
rather than `r num(b0, 3)`. This is the estimate `sensemakr` reports for the
same benchmark. It does not involve the bootstrap.

**Its uncertainty.** The remaining columns come from the bootstrap. In each
of the 1,000 replications `bootmakr` draws firms with replacement, refits the
regression and recomputes the adjusted estimate. The standard deviation of
those 1,000 values is the standard error (`r num(r1$se, 3)`), their 2.5th and
97.5th percentiles form the 95 percent confidence interval
(`r num(r1$ci_lower, 3)` to `r num(r1$ci_upper, 3)`), and the *p*-value
(`r num(r1$pvalue, 3, lead = FALSE)`) is twice the smaller of the two shares
of replications that fall on either side of zero. The pay premium of family
CEOs remains positive and statistically distinguishable from zero even if an
omitted variable as strong as firm age is at work.

**The benchmark strength.** The last block says what "as strong as
`firm_age`" means. Firm age explains `r num(100 * ob$r2dxj.x, 1)` percent of
the variance of the treatment that the other covariates leave unexplained and
`r num(100 * ob$r2yxj.dx, 1)` percent of the remaining variance of the
outcome, which correspond to partial correlations of
`r num(ob$r_dxj.x, 2, lead = FALSE)` and `r num(ob$r_yxj.dx, 2, lead = FALSE)`.
The second table gives the strength of the omitted variable these numbers
imply. All of this is computed from the data. No standard error is involved,
so the block is the same whatever variance estimator you prefer. See
[How strong is the benchmark?](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/benchmark-strength.html)
for the details.

## From one strength to many: the breakdown point

An omitted variable as strong as firm age does not overturn the result. How
much stronger would it have to be? Give `kd` several values and `bootmakr`
evaluates each of them on the same bootstrap samples.

```{r sweep, cache = TRUE}
sweep <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                  benchmark_covariates = "firm_age",
                  kd = seq(0.5, 4, by = 0.5),
                  cluster = "co_code",
                  seed = 123, progress = FALSE)
sweep
```

```{r sweep-plot, fig.cap = "Adjusted estimate and bootstrap 95 percent confidence interval for an omitted variable 0.5 to 4 times as strong as firm age. Solid markers: p < .05."}
plot(sweep, type = "kd_sweep")
```

```{r sweep-numbers, include = FALSE}
rs <- sweep$results
last_sig  <- max(rs$kd[rs$pvalue < 0.05])
first_not <- min(rs$kd[rs$pvalue >= 0.05])
```

The adjusted estimate shrinks as the assumed omitted variable grows stronger,
and its confidence interval widens. In this run the result holds up to an
omitted variable `r last_sig` times as strong as firm age and breaks down at
`r first_not` times: from there on the interval includes zero. That
*breakdown point* is a compact summary of how robust the finding is. (A
*p*-value printed as 0 means that none of the 1,000 replications fell on the
other side of zero.)

A *p*-value this close to .05 can fall on either side of it from one seed to
the next when only 1,000 replications are drawn. Before reporting a
breakdown point, increase `reps` and check that the result has settled. The
[worked example](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/ceo-pay.html)
repeats this sweep with 10,000 replications and puts the breakdown point at
2.5.

## Working with the results

The object returned by `bootmakr()` keeps everything that was computed.

```{r results}
sweep$results
```

`estimate` to `pvalue` are the columns of the printed table. `r2dz.x` and
`r2yz.dx` are the partial R^2^ values of the omitted variable assumed in each
row. The benchmark-strength block is stored as a list of two data frames.

```{r strength}
first$benchmark_strength
```

The bootstrap draws themselves are in `$boot_samples`, one column per value
of `kd`, so the distribution can be inspected without rerunning anything.

```{r histogram, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Bootstrap distribution of the adjusted estimate at kd = 1. The dashed line marks the estimate in the original sample."}
quantile(first$boot_samples[, 1], c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975))

plot(first, type = "histogram")
```

Finally, `$sensemakr_orig` holds the `sensemakr` object of the original fit,
so everything `sensemakr` offers (its summary, robustness values and contour
plots) remains one step away.

## The choices you make

**The benchmark.** Choose an observed covariate that is strong and that you
can argue an omitted variable is unlikely to exceed. Report why you chose it.
A group of covariates can serve as a joint benchmark with
`gbenchmark_covariates = c("x1", "x2")`.

**The multiple.** `kd` scales the benchmark's relationship with the
treatment and `ky` its relationship with the outcome. `ky` follows `kd`
unless you set it.

**The resampling scheme.** It should mirror how the data were sampled and how
you would compute standard errors in the regression itself. Without
`cluster`, rows are resampled, which allows for heteroskedasticity. With
`cluster = "id"`, whole clusters are resampled. `cluster = c("id1", "id2")`
requests two-way clustering, and `strata` a stratified bootstrap. See
[Clustered and stratified data](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/clustering.html).

**Replications and seed.** The default of 1,000 replications is fine for a
first look. For results you report, use more (5,000 or 10,000), set a `seed`,
and when the verdict is close to the significance threshold check that it
has settled with the `converge` argument.

## Where next

- The [worked example](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/ceo-pay.html)
  takes the same study through a complete analysis: the choice of standard
  errors, a stronger benchmark, a grouped benchmark and the convergence
  check.
- `?bootmakr` documents every argument, `?plot.bootmakr` the three plots.
- The Stata command of the same name runs the same analysis. See
  [R and Stata side by side](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/r-and-stata.html).

## References

Chen, G., Chittoor, R., & Vissa, B. (2021). Does nepotism run in the family?
CEO pay and pay-performance sensitivity in Indian family firms. *Strategic
Management Journal*, 42(7), 1326--1343. <https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.3263>

Cinelli, C., & Hazlett, C. (2020). Making sense of sensitivity: Extending
omitted variable bias. *Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series B*,
82(1), 39--67. <https://doi.org/10.1111/rssb.12348>

Lonati, S., & Wulff, J. N. (2026). *Why you should not use the ITCV with
robust standard errors (and what to do instead)*. SSRN working paper.
<https://ssrn.com/abstract=6789678>
